You are writing the objective for a manual test case: a list of the things about this
feature that testing should prove work.

Write it for a tester who knows networking but is new to this feature. Each bullet is one
plain statement of something the feature does, or something that should be true — what you
would say if someone asked "what are we proving here?". Use everyday words and whole
sentences, not stacked technical phrases.

- Stay with the basics of the feature: that it can be set up, that it does what it is for,
  that it refuses settings that are not valid, and that removing it really does remove it.
  This is an overview of the feature working, not a deep-dive into every variation,
  combination or timing.
- Say what should be true, not how to test it. Do NOT write procedural language — no
  "Verify that...", "Configure...", "Set the port...", "Check...". The test steps come later.
- Do NOT name products, platforms, port types, media grades or model numbers, and do NOT
  enumerate exact values or concrete port identifiers. Describe what the feature can do in
  general terms — "each speed the port supports", never an explicit list of speeds, and
  never a specific interface name. The objective must hold on every platform.
- Leave out things that are true of any feature in this area — log entries exist, counters
  increment, management interfaces agree. Keep to what is particular to this feature.
- Use the selections below as evidence of what the feature does, not as a checklist to
  exhaust. Evidence is gathered broadly and will mention adjacent behaviour; include a
  bullet only when it is about THIS case's subject.

## Scope — this case owns one subject
{{ case_key }}{% if case_title %} — {{ case_title }}{% endif %}{% if primary and primary.w %} (primary note: {{ primary.w }}){% endif %}

Write bullets ONLY for that subject.
{%- if siblings %}
These sibling cases are filed alongside it and OWN the adjacent subjects. Their ground is
theirs: do not write bullets for it, even when the evidence below describes it.
{% for s in siblings %}- {{ s.key }}: {{ s.title }}
{% endfor %}
A bullet that would sit just as naturally under one of those siblings belongs to the
sibling, not here. Where this case's subject genuinely depends on a sibling's (a duplex
setting needs a link, a polarity setting needs a speed), state it as the CONDITION the
bullet holds under — never as a bullet of its own.
{%- else %}
No sibling cases were found for this folder. Stay within the subject named above and do not
broaden into adjacent features.
{%- endif %}

